            },
            {
              "id": "2ZYVr",
              "text": "$\\mathrm{CO}+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "$\\mathrm{C}(\\mathrm{s})+\\frac{1}{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}(\\mathrm{g}) ; \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ increases.\r\n\r\nHence, as the temperature increases, $\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S}$ increases and hence $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}(\\Delta \\mathrm{H}-\\mathrm{T} \\Delta \\mathrm{S})$ decreases. In other words, the slope of the curve for formation of CO decreases. However, for all other oxides, it increases.",
